## D841 — OWNER (2026-09-21 08:26): DATA-01 Luna labelling hard cap = $500 total

Verbatim: "500" to the question "what total hard spending limit for Luna labelling, pilot and retries included: $300 (codex) or $500 (supervisor recommendation)?". Ruling: the DATA-01 hard cap is **$500 total**, inclusive of the 200-state pilot, retries, ambiguity re-asks and recollection, with the plan's stop-before-cap rule (plan item 11) binding. The cost band in the plan ($55.50–$285 per 5,000 states at Luna list price, reasoning billed as output) fits under the cap with headroom for the pilot to settle the reasoning-length term. The DATA-01 plan's owner-input placeholder for the cap is to be filled in the next fix round (after astra r2, dispatch on codex reopen 2026-09-22 17:42). Next owner question (one at a time): the DM style Luna should imitate when labelling (recommendation: strongest legal monster tactics). Next free id: D842.

## D844 — OWNER (2026-09-21 09:41): DATA-01 DM style = strongest legal monster tactics; opus (high) and sol (high) judge Luna's actions

Verbatim: "use Strongest legal monster tactics. we will want opus (high) and sol (high) to act as judges of luna's actions". Rulings as read: (a) the DATA-01 teacher rubric (plan §10 Q1, r1 P3-3) is STRONGEST LEGAL MONSTER TACTICS — monsters play to win within the rules, no self-handicap, no flavour or balance softening; this is the versioned style prompt every label is collected under, and the human audit's "acceptable" score is scored against it. (b) A MODEL JUDGE PANEL is added to DATA-01: Claude Opus at high effort (currently routed opus 5 → opus 4.8 per the 2026-09-08 routing) and gpt-5.6-sol at high, each judging Luna's chosen actions against the rubric. Supervisor reading, to be confirmed in the plan's next fix round: the judges SUPPLEMENT the plan's blinded human audit (200 cards; pilot gate still requires a named auditor) — they do not replace it, because the owner said "judges of luna's actions", not "instead of the auditor"; judge verdicts are blinded the same way (identities and option order hidden), recorded as separate columns with their own prompt version, and a judge–teacher disagreement feeds the same stop/quarantine/relabel rule as an auditor "unacceptable". Note against D587.1 (opus 4.8 notes only, no scores, on the plan-review judge panel): that ruling governs PLAN reviews; this is a dataset-label judge, a different job, so opus scoring here is not a conflict but is recorded so nobody reads D587.1 as overriding it. OPEN (next question, one at a time): judge coverage and budget — every labelled state, or the audit sample plus all teacher-vs-engine disagreements (supervisor recommendation, keeps opus spend in the tens of dollars rather than hundreds; sol runs on the codex plan). Both items go into the DATA-01 fix r2 brief with the $500 cap (D841), dispatch when codex reopens 2026-09-22 17:42. Next free id: D845.

## D845 — OWNER (2026-09-21 09:42): judge scores are training signal — judges cover EVERY labelled state

Verbatim: "we can use the judges scores as further information to use for training." Ruling as read: the opus (high) and sol (high) judge verdicts (D844) are not an audit-only sample; they are collected for every labelled state and stored as dataset columns so the training unit can use them (candidate uses, for the plan to decide: per-row quality weight, auxiliary target, disagreement flag for hard-example mining; the plan must state which and must keep judge columns out of the primary label so the model is not trained on a judge's leak of the answer). Consequences the fix r2 brief must carry: (1) judge calls scale with the corpus, so the Opus judge needs its OWN hard cap, separate from the $500 Luna cap (D841); supervisor estimate at opus 4.8 list price ($5/M in, $25/M out): one judge call per state at ≈10k input + ≈2k output ≈ $0.10 → ≈$500 for 5,000 states, ≈$715 at the plan's 7,150-state ceiling; sol judging runs on the codex plan at no marginal dollar cost but at codex usage-limit cost (D839 shows that limit is real). (2) Judge prompt is versioned like the teacher prompt; identities/option order blinded; judge sees the rubric (strongest legal monster tactics) and the engine's ranking is WITHHELD from the judge so its score is independent evidence. (3) The human audit (200 cards, named auditor) stays as the pilot gate; the judges do not replace it (D844 assumption, now reinforced: a judge that is also training signal cannot be its own gate). OPEN (next question, one at a time): the Opus judge's separate hard cap — supervisor recommendation $750 total. Next free id: D846.

## D846 — OWNER (2026-09-21 09:43): Luna re-runs each state under several strategy tunings; objective = maximize chance of winning

Verbatim: "have luna re-run each several times trying different strategy tunings. this will help us best uncover the best strategies given that the goal is to maximize the chance of winning". Rulings as read: (a) per labelled state, Luna is called under SEVERAL distinct strategy tunings (a small fixed, versioned set of named strategy prompts, all inside the strongest-legal-tactics style of D844 — e.g. focus-fire the weakest, focus-fire the highest threat, control/disable first, maximize area damage, conserve-and-attrit, regroup/retreat; the exact set is the plan's to propose and astra's to review), each sample tagged with its tuning id; (b) the OBJECTIVE is now chosen: chance of winning. The plan's §outcome sidecar said "do not collapse to a scalar until the owner chooses a utility" — the owner has chosen it: win probability (survival/unresolved and censoring rules as already written). Supervisor reading, to be confirmed in fix r2: the "best strategy" per state is decided by the engine rollout sidecar (32 seeds per action to terminal or horizon), not by a judge's opinion — the simulator is the ground truth for win chance; judges (D844/D845) score legality/reasoning and become auxiliary columns; the dataset keeps every tuning's action, its simulated win vector, and the judge scores, with the primary label = the action with the highest simulated win probability among Luna's tuned choices (ties and censored buckets handled per the existing rules). Cost consequence under the $500 Luna cap (D841): calls per state multiply by the tuning count; at the plan's $0.012 per-call reserve, 4 tunings × 3 samples = $0.144/state ≈ 2,980 states vs ≈ 7,150 at 5 samples — the fix round must present the state-count/tuning trade-off table and the owner picks; the rollout sidecar's compute (32 seeds × actions × tunings × states) also grows and needs its own wall-clock estimate on the box. The Opus judge cap question (D845, rec. $750) remains the open owner question. All of D841/D844/D845/D846 go into the DATA-01 fix r2 brief; dispatch when codex reopens 2026-09-22 17:42. Next free id: D847.

