## Familywise-control and simulation check

The revised Luna proof is valid: if an emitted leader is not a true best option, at least one preregistered ordered null `H(leader,j): p_leader ≤ p_j` is true and must have been rejected. Holm controls those ordered hypotheses at 0.0125 per look; Bonferroni over at most four looks gives FWER ≤0.05. The observed direction is therefore included.

The rollout rule is implementable without inventing an interval algorithm: ordered one-sided Fisher tests, Holm across `K(K−1)`, alpha 0.0125/look, unique empirical leader after adjustment, successful looks `64/128/256/512`, and a 640-attempt ceiling per candidate. Ceiling or non-separation emits `none_dominant`.

I reproduced the plan’s deterministic Monte Carlo exactly:

| Configuration | False hard labels | Rate | MC SE |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| K=2, equal | 383/20,000 | 0.019150 | 0.000969 |
| K=2, two tied leaders | 354/20,000 | 0.017700 | 0.000932 |
| K=5, equal | 0/20,000 | 0 | 0 |
| K=5, two tied leaders | 23/20,000 | 0.001150 | 0.000240 |
| K=18, equal | 0/20,000 | 0 | 0 |
| K=18, two tied leaders | 2/20,000 | 0.000100 | 0.000071 |
| Own check: K=3, `(.34,.33,.33)` | 6/20,000 | 0.000300 | 0.000122 |

The near-tie run emitted any hard label 8/20,000 (`0.000400`, SE `0.000141`); six selected a non-best option. Total elapsed time, including the added configuration, was 0.322 seconds.

